1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,821
Can an artery narrow again inside a stent?

2
00:00:03,425 --> 00:00:08,266
This simulated angiogram shows a narrower
channel where a stent was placed.

3
00:00:08,963 --> 00:00:12,748
Finding the cause helps the team decide
what to do next.

4
00:00:13,444 --> 00:00:18,448
In-stent restenosis means narrowing that
develops or persists within or

5
00:00:18,517 --> 00:00:20,793
close to a stented section.

6
00:00:21,397 --> 00:00:25,089
A stent may still be in place when the
channel becomes narrow.

7
00:00:25,785 --> 00:00:29,547
Some people notice symptoms such as chest
discomfort returning.

8
00:00:30,058 --> 00:00:33,669
The team considers the symptoms and the
test findings together.

9
00:00:34,365 --> 00:00:36,571
There are several possible causes.

10
00:00:37,082 --> 00:00:40,507
The stent may have remained partly
compressed when it was put in.

11
00:00:41,018 --> 00:00:44,210
Tissue may grow inside a stent that opened
well.

12
00:00:44,907 --> 00:00:48,622
New plaque can also develop in tissue
inside the stent.

13
00:00:49,319 --> 00:00:54,206
These are different problems, even if the
angiogram shows a narrow channel in each

14
00:00:54,264 --> 00:00:54,891
case.

15
00:00:55,588 --> 00:00:59,210
A clot in a stent is called stent
thrombosis.

16
00:00:59,651 --> 00:01:03,181
It can suddenly block blood flow and cause
a heart attack.

17
00:01:03,993 --> 00:01:08,997
Restenosis and a clot need different
assessment, and symptoms alone cannot tell

18
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:14,071
which has happened. Severe chest pressure
or sudden trouble breathing are

19
00:01:14,106 --> 00:01:16,277
reasons to get emergency help.

20
00:01:16,881 --> 00:01:21,849
The team may use contrast X-rays to see
where the channel is narrow and how the

21
00:01:21,919 --> 00:01:23,451
rest of the artery looks.

22
00:01:24,055 --> 00:01:27,085
The outline alone may leave the cause
unclear.

23
00:01:27,689 --> 00:01:32,020
Reviewing the previous procedure and
earlier images helps put the new findings

24
00:01:32,066 --> 00:01:33,076
in context.

25
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:37,964
The team can sometimes use imaging from
inside the artery to see the stent and the

26
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:39,961
vessel wall in more detail.

27
00:01:40,565 --> 00:01:43,560
Intravascular ultrasound uses sound.

28
00:01:44,164 --> 00:01:47,449
Optical coherence tomography uses light.

29
00:01:48,262 --> 00:01:52,836
These images can help show whether the
mesh expanded properly and whether tissue

30
00:01:52,883 --> 00:01:54,787
has narrowed the space inside.

31
00:01:55,390 --> 00:02:00,127
If the stent did not expand well, the team
considers how to address that mechanical

32
00:02:00,197 --> 00:02:05,479
problem. For selected cases, treatment may
include a drug-coated balloon or

33
00:02:05,537 --> 00:02:07,186
another drug-eluting stent.

34
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:10,622
A coated balloon is removed after
treatment.

35
00:02:11,226 --> 00:02:15,429
A new drug-eluting stent leaves another
scaffold at the treated site.

36
00:02:16,032 --> 00:02:20,688
The care team can explain what it found,
what treatment is appropriate and how the

37
00:02:20,758 --> 00:02:22,464
follow-up plan changes.

38
00:02:23,277 --> 00:02:27,399
Useful questions are what caused the
narrowing, how the team checked it, and

39
00:02:27,457 --> 00:02:30,464
which medicines or follow-up visits are
part of the plan.

40
00:02:31,161 --> 00:02:34,179
Those answers connect the finding to the
next step.
